def projtxt():
    d = PR['ssp585|2071-2100']; b = PR['ssp245|2071-2100']; nl = R3['proj_nl']['ssp585|2071-2100']; tc = R3['temporal_climate_slope']
    return [f"Because the transferred effect varies with climate, we asked how it would change if each city's present surface experienced the climates projected for 2071–2100 by five CMIP6 models (NEX-GDDP-CMIP6)[@thrasher2022,eyring2016,oneill2016]. Although warm-season maximum temperature rose by a median {m(b['dT_median'], 1)} °C under SSP2-4.5 and {m(d['dT_median'], 1)} °C under SSP5-8.5, the heat cost of ten percentage points of sealing in fringe cells barely changed ({m(d['d_tau_median'], 3)} °C under SSP5-8.5; Fig. 5a,b). This is a property of the effect function rather than a forecast: once tree cover and the greenness of the rural background are modifiers, climate adds little to the variation of the effect between cities. Three checks support the direction. The nonlinear transfer model gave {m(nl['median'], 3)} °C, a model with climate-only modifiers {m(d['d_tau_alt_median'], 3)} °C, and within cities, across the four epochs, the effect changed by {m(tc[0])} °C per °C of warm-season temperature (95% CI {rng(tc[1], tc[2])} °C). Warming alone is therefore unlikely to make sealing much costlier for surface heat; what sealing removes, and how future climate and irrigation alter that vegetation, matter more."]
