DISC = [
 "A model that predicts urban heat well is not necessarily a model whose perturbations answer a sealing policy's question. Gradient boosting with vegetation among its predictors predicted four decades of change best, yet the sealing effects implied by pooled predictive models depended on their conditioning set and ranked cities worse than a causal transfer model. Within cities, the same algorithm returned the direct effect when vegetation was held fixed and the total effect only when vegetation was shifted with sealing. Which variables a model conditions on decides which contrast its perturbations describe, predictive accuracy does not reveal it[@westreich2013,zhao2021], and only orthogonalized estimators address the confounding they are given[@chernozhukov2018]; no design here validates the effect size against an experiment.",
 "For sustainability policy the estimand matters because it changes both the size of the heat cost and where it is largest. Land-take rules such as the European Union's principle of minimizing sealing[@eusml2025] and urban greening targets concern total effects: sealing with its loss of vegetation, and sealing of neighbourhoods rather than single cells. On our estimates, the heat cost of a sealing decision is set mainly by the vegetation that sealing removes, is largest in temperate and continental cities and in fringes and rural land where vegetation is lost, and is absent in arid ones; it will change little with warming alone. A planner screening cities or districts with a predictive model's perturbation would misjudge that cost by more than its size and misorder the cities; a causal transfer model orders them better but still needs local verification with new satellite epochs, dated construction records or air-temperature observations. We therefore propose separating three layers (Fig. 5c): predictive models for mapping heat, stated interventions with confounding adjustment for effects, and decisions that combine effects and their uncertainty with everything else that matters to a city[@elmqvist2019,mcdonald2026].",
]
LIMS = ("Six limitations bound these conclusions. First, Landsat measures clear-sky surface temperature in the mid-morning; air temperature, night-time heat and thermal comfort respond differently[@venter2021,voogt2003,krayenhoff2018,mcdonald2026], and the overpass drifted by about half an hour over four decades. Second, identification rests on terrain, location and, in the lagged design, pre-existing heat; the negative control and sensitivity bounds show that unmeasured confounding such as irrigation, land management or redevelopment remains plausible, and irrigation of urban greenery may explain part of the absent effect in arid cities. Third, impervious and tree cover before 2021 are back-cast from Landsat spectra, and confirmation by dated conversion depends on the dating product. Fourth, sealing spills over to neighbouring cells, so cell-level effects understate the cost of sealing a neighbourhood, and cores contribute fewer cells than rural land. Fifth, the purposive, geographically balanced selection does not represent the world's urban population. Sixth, the transfer model is linear in its modifiers and transports effects only within the support of present-day climates[@pearl2014,meyer2021], and the projections hold surfaces and vegetation fixed. The most valuable next steps are designs with independently dated construction, such as building permits or time-stamped footprints, and validation with air-temperature networks.")
CONCL = (f"Predictive accuracy alone cannot validate the effect of sealing that a model implies. Under stated identification assumptions, sealing warmed daytime surfaces mainly by removing vegetation, by between about {m(S['sens_adj_pool']['mu'], 2)} and {m(tot['mu'], 2)} °C per ten percentage points in the average city-region, more in temperate and continental cities and not in arid ones, with additional warming of the surroundings. Pooled predictive models misjudged this cost and its ranking across cities; a causal transfer model ordered cities better and can support screening of where limits on sealing matter most, provided local estimates confirm it.")

